                <pb facs="0897" n="804" sameAs="frus:frus1935v04/pg804"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.404/1750</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Mexico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Daniels</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-07-11T14:00:00-06:00">July 11, 1935—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 6:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>119. In the course of a conversation with the President yesterday I informed him
                    of the interest of President Roosevelt and the American Government in the
                    Mexican religious situation. He stated that his administration was primarily
                    interested in economical and educational matters and in regard to religion that
                    it intended to observe the religious laws. He did not indicate that he
                    contemplated making any statement regarding religious policy but said that he
                    had heard that several states might adopt a more moderate attitude.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Daniels</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
